  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ハスイモ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%8F%E3%82%B9%E3%82%A4%E3%83%A2",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 110317885,
      "text": "ハスイモ は、サトイモ科サトイモ属の常緑性多年草。ヒマラヤから中国、日本などで栽培されているズイキ（葉柄）専用種である。別名はシロイモ。サトイモの近縁種で、分類上は別種であるが、栽培上はサトイモの同類として扱われる。塊根は小さくて食用にならないが、長い葉柄の芋茎、茎長80センチメートル (cm) が食用になる。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Leucocasia gigantea」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leucocasia_gigantea",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1369152681,
      "text": "Leucocasia gigantea, also called the giant elephant ear or Indian taro, is a species of flowering plant. It is a 1.5–3 m tall aroid plant with a large, fibrous corm, producing at its apex a whorl of thick, green leaves. It is the sole species in genus Leucocasia."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Leucocasia gigantea」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leucocasia_gigantea#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1369152681,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Leucocasia gigantea is a \"sister species\" to another widely-cultivated 'taro', Colocasia esculenta, as well as to the alocasias, such as the large Alocasia macrorrhizos; it is speculated that L. gigantea was created as a result of natural hybridization between A. macrorrhizos and C. esculenta. It is called 'dọc mùng' in northern Vietnam and 'môn bạc hà' or 'bạc hà' in some provinces in southern Vietnam.\nIn Japanese, it is commonly called ハス芋 (hasu-imo), or \"lotus yam\". It is known as ryukyu in Kōchi Prefecture, as it is found in the Ryukyu Kingdom.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
